ities to tissue sampling accuracy\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Guided biopsy is described as using imaging (ultrasound or CT) to direct needle placement, including for lymph node biopsy. This positions imaging not only as diagnostic visualization but also as procedural navigation infrastructure. [Data: Entities (4545); Relationships (9691)]\\n\\nThe community includes a broader concept of imaging-guided biopsy, where mammogram, ultrasound, or MRI can be used to accurately sample a target area. This creates a cross-modality bridge between imaging and intervention. [Data: Entities (3778); Relationships (7784)]\\n\\nWithin prostate care, ultrasound images are specifically described as enabling clinicians to guide a prostate biopsy by directing needle placement to remove tissue samples without opening the abdomen, and transrectal ultrasound (TRUS) imaging is linked to the production of ultrasound images that provide an internal view of the prostate for biopsy sampling guidance. [Data: Entities (3464); Relationships (6961)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Early-stage localized prostate cancer is framed as highly treatable, with early detection tied to strong outcomes\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Early-stage (localized) prostate cancer is defined as disease that has not spread beyond the prostate gland, typically slow growing, contained entirely within the prostate, and highly treatable and often curable. The relationships further specify treatment options including surgery, radiation therapy, and hormone therapy. [Data: Entities (3196); Relationships (6317)]\\n\\nThe dataset also emphasizes outcomes: when prostate cancer is found early (early-stage), it is stated to have one of the highest survival rates of any cancer. In this community context, that makes early detection and appropriate management pathways (including active surveillance in select low-risk cases) particularly consequential for decision-makers. [Data: Relationships (6320); Entities (3210, 3201)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Thyroid nodule evaluation forms a structured pathway: testing, imaging, and selective biopsy/monitoring\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Thyroid nodules are described as small, often round areas of abnormal growth; most are benign and asymptomatic, but some can be cancerous and may require testing (including a TSH blood test), ultrasound, and possibly biopsy. This anchors a stepwise evaluation approach grounded in both laboratory assessment and imaging characterization. [Data: Entities (4993, 5012, 5011); Relationships (10997, 11001, 11002)]\\n\\nThe data adds decision-support layers for indeterminate cases via molecular tests, which can help determine whether a nodule is cancerous and whether surgery is necessary. It also describes a non-invasive management option for some nodules that do not need biopsy: monitoring over time with repeated ultrasound evaluation. [Data: Entities (5002, 5019); Relationships (10999, 11003)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Ultrasound is positioned as first-line imaging for suspected ovarian cancer and can assess nearby tissues\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Within imaging testing for suspected ovarian cancer, ultrasound is described as the typical first imaging test. This places ultrasound at the front of the diagnostic funnel for pelvic malignancy suspicion. [Data: Entities (2166, 1907); Relationships (3717)]\\n\\nThe relationships further state that ultrasound can show nearby tissues around pelvic organs to help evaluate disease spread or masses. This highlights a capability relevant to staging considerations and broader pelvic assessment, even when the initial diagnostic question is ovarian. [Data: Entities (2069, 1907); Relationships (3490)]\\n\\nTransabdominal ultrasound is also represented as a specific technique (gel spread on the abdomen/pelvis with the probe guided over the skin) to obtain pelvic images, linking procedural method to the pelvic imaging use-case. [Data: Entities (2070, 1546); Relationships (3496, 3501)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Echocardiography links ultrasound mechanics to structural and functional heart assessment (including ejection fraction)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"An echocardiogram (ECHO) is described as an ultrasound-based cardiac test that uses sound waves to create moving images of the beating heart, with real-time viewing on a screen and recording for later review. This connects the general ultrasound mechanism to a specialized cardiac application with live functional visualization. [Data: Entities (1881, 1884, 1544); Relationships (3180, 3181)]\\n\\nThe dataset specifies that echocardiography evaluates heart structure, including valves and muscle thickness. These elements represent the structural assessment dimension of the community’s cardiac branch. [Data: Entities (1905, 1906); Relationships (3184, 3185)]\\n\\nOn the functional side, ejection fraction is defined as how much blood the left side of the heart pumps out with each heartbeat, and a lower-than-normal ejection fraction is said to indicate decreased heart function. Relatedly, left ventricular ejection fraction (LVEF) is defined as blood pumping from the left side of the heart, and LVEF tests measure this value. [Data: Entities (1885, 1886, 1908); Relationships (3189, 3190, 8396)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Procedure setup details (gel use, patient positioning, rectal gel insertion) are explicit enablers of image quality\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Gel is repeatedly characterized as a coupling substance used to improve contact and signal transmission between an imaging device and the body, and is applied to the patient’s skin and/or the probe tip to eliminate air gaps and enhance image clarity. The relationships also tie gel use concretely to transabdominal ultrasound (gel applied/spread on abdomen and pelvis to make images clearer). [Data: Entities (1546, 2070); Relationships (3501, 3496)]\\n\\nFor thyroid/neck ultrasound, the datase